  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nCurrent satellite shows progressing mid to high level clouds \nassociated with southwesterly flow aloft leading to persisting dry \nconditions. The flow turns more zonal this evening, a brief decrease \nin coverage in cloud cover overnight is expected. On Thursday, our \nattention shifts to the Pacific coast as another atmospheric river \nbegins to move inland. Persistent ridging off the southwest US coast \nwill steer the flow into a more northwesterly component Thursday \nnight into Friday morning. This will lead to increasing chances for \nsnow for both the central mountains, particularly around the Stanley \narea. and the eastern highlands. Snow levels will remain above \n6000ft. The NBM highlights mean QPF amounts on Friday that are \naround 0.25 to 0.5 inches, with greater than an inch of QPF possible \nin the higher peaks. Galena Summit will begin to experience minor \nimpacts starting Friday evening.\n\nThe primary shortwave in the flow will pass through the region \nFriday night, increasing winds and influencing where the bulk of the \nprecipitation will fall. QPF values through Friday night into \nSaturday early afternoon are in the 0.5 to 0.75 inch range, while \nhigher elevations are projected at over an inch of QPF. Due to the \nNW orientation of the flow, shadowing will occur for much of the \nSnake River plain. 72 hour QPF values are generally less than 0.1 \ninch but increase closer to the southeast highlands where upslope \nflow will favor slightly higher totals around 0.25 inches. By \nSaturday morning, precipitation chances will be diminishing for the \nCentral mountains while remaining for the eastern highlands until \naround mid day. Snow totals around Galena Summit are ranging from 3 \nto 7 inches. Current guidance suggests a greater than 90 percent \nchance of 3 or more inches of snow, with a less than 20 percent \nchance of 7 or more inches. Passes in the eastern highlands should \ntop out in the 1 to 3 inch range.\n\nA lull in precipitation is expected late Saturday and much of \nSunday. Our break from active weather will be brief. Ensembles are \nin agreement that another trough of low pressure will move into the \nregion from the west late Sunday night into Monday morning. There is \nstill some spread in depth and magnitude, resulting in some \nuncertainty for the potential for more widespread precipitation. The \npattern following looks to remain active as ensembles display daily \nprecipitation chances for much of next week.",
